      "chapter": "General Information",
      "table_no": null,
      "title": "[Synoptic table of the Cenozoic history of China]",
      "caption_extra": "Section: Geology. Geology—Cenozoic. \"The following synoptic table shows the present state of knowledge in regard to the Cenozoic history of China:\" (introduced at foot of p. 30)",
      "parts": [
        {
          "label": null,
          "columns": [
            "Geological age.",
            "Name of the formation.",
            "Lithologic character.",
            "Fossil contents.",
            "Distribution."
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Pleistocene.",
              "Loess.",
              "Loess.",
              "Helicadae shells, ostrich eggs, tusks & molars of Elephant.",
              "Widely distributed all over N. China."
            ],
            [
              "Lower Pleistocene.",
              "Sanmen series.",
              "Sands & gravels.",
              "Fresh water molluscas as Quadrula & Cuneopsis.",
              "Sanmen Rapids on the Yellow River, Honan; also N. of Ordos."
            ],
            [
              "Pliocene.",
              "Hipparion clay. Prob. also the \"Deck sandstein\" of S. China.",
              "Red and variegated clays with layers of gravels (often conglomeratic).",
              "Hipparion, Rhinoceros, Deer, Antelope, Hyena, etc.; also Suidae, Probascidae, Cynocephalus, etc.",
              "Widely distributed in N. China. The lignite deposits of E. Yunnan containing plant fossils and gastropoda are probably of the same age."
            ],
            [
              "Oligocene.",
              "Fushun coal series.",
              "Coal series interbedded with basaltic lavas. The coal is lignitic to subbituminous.",
              "Plant fossils: Osmunda, Sequoia, Glyptostrabus, Populus, Fugus etc.",
              "Fushun and Shihmenchai in S. Manchuria. Also N. Shensi & inner Mongolia."
            ],
            [
              "Eocene.",
              "Yuanchu series in Honan & Kuanchang series in Shantung.",
              "Thick conglomerate interbedded with sandstones, clays and marls.",
              "Planorbis, Physa Euchilus, Eupera and bones of reptiles and mammals.",
              "Widely distributed in N. China from Kansu to Shantung."
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "transcriber_notes": [
        "Text table; line-end hyphenations in the narrow columns joined up (e.g. \"mol-luscas\" -> \"molluscas\"). Spellings kept as printed (Helicadae, Probascidae, Glyptostrabus, Fugus, \"Deck sandstein\")."
      ],
